module bindings.python.django_authenticate_without_request;

// Django's authenticate() runs every configured authentication backend and
// returns the user whose credentials matched. Since Django 1.11 the call
// takes the request as its first argument, and the contract is documented in
// two places at once: Django's own authentication docs pass
// `authenticate(request=request, ...)`, and the brute-force protection that
// layers onto this hook -- django-axes is the canonical one, installed as an
// authentication backend and a middleware -- reads the client's address and
// user agent off that request to count and lock out attempts. A backend that
// limits attempts by client identity can only do its job when the request
// reaches it; a credential verification made without the request is counted
// nowhere and throttled by nothing request-scoped, so the endpoint that
// routes it can be driven at machine speed.
//
// The binding reports the call itself. `callee.path ==` keeps it to Django's
// documented function, resolved through the usual
// `from django.contrib.auth import authenticate` import; a credential
// submission spelled as a method on some other object -- an IMAP client's
// `proto.authenticate(...)`, a PAM helper, a test harness's
// `self.authenticate()` -- does not resolve to that path and stays with the
// bindings that judge those APIs. The negation reads the call's own
// arguments, so `authenticate(username=..., password=...)` is flagged while
// `authenticate(request=request, ...)` is not; the request passed
// positionally declines too, because the argument tokens a binding sees are
// flow-expanded and carry the request expression's own text. The value
// tokens a keyword contributes are its name, which is why the request's
// keyword is visible here and a credentials-only call is not.
//
// VYQL-AUTH-105 consumes the label and clears it wherever a rate-limit
// control covers the endpoint. Residual, stated rather than hidden: a
// request-less authenticate in a management command or fixture generator --
// the demo-data creator in a Django project is the usual one, authenticating
// a user it just created with generated credentials -- carries no
// attacker-driven attempt surface but is flagged with the endpoint it would
// have, one review flag per project; and the credentials-only positional
// spelling `authenticate(user, password)` is flagged as well, which is the
// pre-1.11 idiom and the same uncounted attempt either way.
binding djangoAuthenticateWithoutRequest {
  query pattern callExpr where callee.path == "django.contrib.auth.authenticate"
    and not args.any.literal contains "request"
  emit issue code.RateLimitRequiredOp at call
}
